#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049091 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/math-academy-id 725 :topic/title "Even and Odd Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033 :topic/math-academy-id 2707 :topic/title "Local Extrema of Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718 :topic/math-academy-id 1491 :topic/title "Graphing Sine and Cosine"}] :topic/id #uuid "d9b41e19-22fc-5fad-93ae-b8f50010d70b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693 :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi.$\n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694 :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-11003" :question/problem "If $\\cos x =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ find the value of $2\\cos( x + 4\\pi)+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x & =cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cos⁡(x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos{x} =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ then $\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} = - \\dfrac {1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2cos⁡(x+4\\pi )+3 & =2(-\\frac{1}{3})+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11012" :question/problem "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4},$ then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x-6\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3557 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049891 :topic/math-academy-id 932 :topic/title "Rotations as Linear Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050359 :topic/math-academy-id 3563 :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050472 :topic/math-academy-id 259 :topic/title "Graphing Reflections of Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050762 :topic/math-academy-id 1827 :topic/title "Products of Even and Odd Functions"}] :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049108 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132 :topic/math-academy-id 245 :topic/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149 :topic/math-academy-id 1275 :topic/title "The Area of a General Triangle"}] :topic/id #uuid "db18b7ba-2bde-56e6-a3f9-5471d37eae2f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105590 :question/id #uuid "957d070a-edc8-521f-a5bd-d10177a0bdbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4344" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,0,-3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 5)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-3i-23j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-23,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-23)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{554}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Triangle Spanned by Two Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105591 :question/id #uuid "e354c43b-837f-5447-9759-6aeb3952cd57" :question/math-academy-id "e-4345" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ if $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(-2,0,1),$ $C(1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+j-k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)i-(-3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0)j+(-3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105592 :question/id #uuid "23793048-621c-5ab3-97ac-7e635553c947" :question/math-academy-id "e-4346" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0),$ $B(1,1,2),$ $C(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1299 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384 :topic/math-academy-id 1790 :topic/title "Areas of Parametric Surfaces"}] :topic/title "Finding Areas Using the Cross Product"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049131 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049406 :topic/math-academy-id 1924 :topic/title "Inhibited Growth Models With First-Order ODEs"}] :topic/id #uuid "dd6da6f3-04b6-5a5c-ad43-d66c3a2a88c4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :topic/math-academy-id 649 :topic/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/math-academy-id 153 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/math-academy-id 116 :topic/title "Solving Radical Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049277 :topic/math-academy-id 4858 :topic/title "Properties of the Cross Product"}] :topic/id #uuid "dd7556b7-73ec-546a-b810-4eb2042fdb48" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059545 :question/id #uuid "d2698dbf-dea6-5abb-970e-a3ace180e337" :question/math-academy-id "e-4341" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,-4,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -7,0,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 0)i-(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-7))j+(3\\cdot 0-(-4)\\cdot (-7))k \\\\  & =-8i-13j-28k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-13,-28⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059546 :question/id #uuid "b834e666-de4f-5964-a280-6571ad44b6bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4343" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3, 1, 2x \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,x \\rangle,$ and $|\\,\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\,| = \\sqrt{19}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2x \\\\ 0 & 1 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2x \\\\ 1 & x\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2x \\\\ 0 & x\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-xi-3xj+3k \\\\  & =⟨-x,-3x,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-x)^{2}+(-3x)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & |\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{19} \\\\  & \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}=\\sqrt{19} \\\\  & 10{x}^{2}+9=19 \\\\  & 10{x}^{2}=10 \\\\  & {x}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x= \\pm 1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in an Equation Involving the Cross Product"}] :topic/math-academy-id 245 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648 :topic/math-academy-id 1836 :topic/title "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049108 :topic/math-academy-id 1299 :topic/title "Finding Areas Using the Cross Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049291 :topic/math-academy-id 1741 :topic/title "Limits of Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049663 :topic/math-academy-id 1744 :topic/title "Integration Rules for Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049898 :topic/math-academy-id 3176 :topic/title "Binormal Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308 :topic/math-academy-id 1806 :topic/title "The Parametric Equations of a Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356 :topic/math-academy-id 1286 :topic/title "The Scalar Triple Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830 :topic/math-academy-id 1807 :topic/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"}] :topic/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}]}
